organizers had built this second weekend  of the blockade around the canadian  parliament as a family day it's clear  there's an awareness of the negative  headlines of last weekend when  protesters were accused of attacking  those wearing masks and displaying  racist symbols  the blockade began as a protest about  cross-border trucking vaccine mandates  even though 90 percent of cross-border  truckers are vaccinated and the u.s  insists on vaccination too but clearly  it's now about pandemic policy in  general we're fed up with these  restrictions since two years  everywhere locked down curfew  you know what you i mean  and those who joined the protesters on  saturday also raised a range of issues  the protest organizers who are from the  extreme right wing of canadian politics  are demanding the overthrow of the  government but there were also  anti-vaxxers religious fundamentalists  and those raising topics of concern to  both the right and left like the power  of pharmaceutical companies or the civil  liberties implications of mandates and  then there were those who'd simply had  enough of the pandemic everything has  been locked down in person lock down  again  spending like days and weeks in my  bedroom and then going all the way back  to school and still feeling isolated the  polls do show a majority of canadians  are fed up with the handling of the  pandemic by both federal and provincial  authorities but they also show  overwhelming support for mandates and  public health measures designed to ease  the strain on the health care system  and that makes the organizers ultimatum  that they won't budge unless all  mandates are removed challenging  it is difficult to see how this will end  we've covered protests in canada in the  past either for indigenous rights or  against pipelines and the authorities  have cracked down swiftly but these  trucks aren't going to be moved so  easily shia brutality al jazeera ottawa
 

